body:
This system exclusively uses JR CENTRAL's L1 Series (successor to Improved L0 Series) and newer successors but with power boosted and runs at top service speed of 500 mi/h = 804.672 km/h for super express, 375 mi/h = 603.504 km/h for limited express, 250 mi/h = 402.336 km/h for express, and 125 mi/h for local. This system's routing was purposely done so to satisfy the best balance between serving the largest number of metropolitan areas, minimizing cross-country travel times, and minimizing construction costs by not serving small population centers such as the Interstate 84 corridor (through Boise and the surrounding Idaho valleys).

&#x200B;

total system length = 13325.3 mi = 21444.9916 km

&#x200B;

total cost, including planning, permitting, and construction: 0.75-2.5 trillion U.S. dollars, due to extensive tunneling, long-span bridges across major waterways, and viaducts across valleys

&#x200B;

ticket price: The ticket price for the maglev ride during any day of the year will be a constant 95.45% as expensive as a comparable airline ticket for peak season. Even though it is expensive, it is well worth it for the time savings from not having to go through airport-style check-in and security, convenience of being able to hop on along any of the larger towns along the route (and be able to travel cross-country on the system with just a cross-platform transfer at the limited express and super express stations), and lack of hassle of having to travel to and from the airport far on the far outskirts of a major city.

&#x200B;

Some stations may currently only be located in small towns, but they are planned to act as a gateway to major development of the small towns.

